=== Grades 4-5 Problem 2: the flip map of the 1,2,2 hexagon ===
PASS map has 6 ways (dots) and 6 one-flip lines: LLRR-LRLR, LRLR-LRRL, LRLR-RLLR, LRRL-RLRL, RLLR-RLRL, RLRL-RRLL
INFO number of flips possible from each way (by its chain letters): {'RRLL': 1, 'RLRL': 3, 'RLLR': 2, 'LRRL': 2, 'LRLR': 3, 'LLRR': 1}
PASS farthest-apart pair needs 4 flips: [('LLRR', 'RRLL')] (the only pair that far apart)
PASS the map has exactly one loop (edges - dots + 1 = 1): the square RLRL-RLLR-LRLR-LRRL

=== Grades 4-5 Problem 3: chains (ribbons) in the 1,2,2 hexagon ===
PASS the six ways have six different chains, and they are exactly the six orders of L, L, R, R: ['LLRR', 'LRLR', 'LRRL', 'RLLR', 'RLRL', 'RRLL']
PASS every flip swaps one neighbouring L and R in the chain (LR <-> RL), and nothing else in the chain changes

=== Grades 4-5 Problem 4: counting with chains ===
PASS 45-P4a hexagon (1, 3, 2): 10 ways = number of orders of 3 R and 2 L = C(5,3); every way has a different chain
PASS 45-P4b hexagon (1, 3, 3): 20 ways = number of orders of 3 R and 3 L = C(6,3); every way has a different chain
PASS 45-P4c hexagon (1, 4, 4): 70 ways = number of orders of 4 R and 4 L = C(8,4); every way has a different chain
PASS Pascal pattern: 10 = 4 + 6, 20 = 10 + 10, and the 1,4,4 hexagon has 70 ways

